From 5ec5c33c6248fa02afc7c4fed6f7a6d860430777 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 03:39:55 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] dvbridge: the tile mask of a GUI texture, made from the
 texture itself

For the composite of the GUI over HDR10 and HLG there is no packer of the source
transport to make the tiles of pass 7. dvbridge_guimask makes the mask from the
GUI texture: two small draws as pass 6 does them (4 x 4 texels for each fragment
of a quarter of its size, then of that: about 8.8 million texel reads for a 4K
texture, a little over half of what the whole quad of the composite reads and
discards), the result read back into a pixel pack buffer with a fence and taken
without waiting. A tile is marked where a texel of it, or of one of the eight
tiles around it, has an alpha above 0, which covers every pixel whose bilinear
sample can take such a texel (dvbridge_tiles.h says why the quad under glScissor
rectangles of the mask is the same bytes). The GUI texture is read with its own
nearest, clamped sampler object: texelFetch of a texture whose filter wants
mipmaps reads nothing. GL state is saved and restored.

Cost against saving. The mask is made once for a content of the GUI that stays
for a frame; the first frame of a content whose GUI pass is left out asks for it.
At 2 Hz updates of the player information a content lasts about 30 frames at 60
Hz, and each frame after the mask comes back (one or two) draws about 5 to 20 %
of the quad: the mask costs about half a quad, once, and saves most of a quad in
each of about 28 frames.

Host proof (outside the tree), perf_bridge with DV_SCISSOR_HDR: Kodi's own
gles_gui_composite shaders, the ordinary projection and blend, 8-bit targets of
3840x2160, 2560x1440, 1920x1080 and 1366x768 (not a multiple of 16), the whole
quad and the quad under the rectangles of this mask over the same picture and a
GUI with a gradient of alpha, lines of one pixel, single pixels at the corners
and on tile edges, a noisy block and a block that ends on a tile edge, compared
byte by byte: 0 pixels differ on llvmpipe and on zink over lavapipe at every
size; with each rectangle one tile short, 2 or 3 differ at every size on both.
